export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p>Because it only took a Playstation 2 for the Xbox team to join the console war.</p>\n<p>These two titans have fought for nearly two decades. And they will be fighting again with the coming of Playstation 5 and Xbox Series X later this year. We may not know who will come out victorious, but we do know how they will compete. Let’s take look at the technologies powering these two consoles.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/258703a51f66f1db1c9b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/258703a51f66f1db1c9b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"578\" style=\"--source-width:313px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/37209ba42ddb64f90270.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/37209ba42ddb64f90270.webp\" width=\"814\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:254px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Photogrammetry</strong></p>\n<p>For years, game studios have yearned to create life-like assets. The steady rise of photogrammetry in recent times has a positive impact on this pursuit overall—especially with the ever-expanding library of Quixel. Newer consoles. Stronger hardware. More realistic graphics. It’s the first thing coming to our minds.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>UE5</strong></p>\n<p>Last week, the developer showed their 5<sup>th</sup> iteration of the renowned Unreal Engine, running in real-time on a PS5. This latest version incorporates a new feature called “Nanite”, which can virtualize hundreds of billion polygons simultaneously. With this technology, artists can now focus on making their assets at the highest quality with no polycount concerns.</p>\n<p>The result is just remarkable.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/a216b4690aae539dfd77.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/a216b4690aae539dfd77.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:254px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/243ddff62e10b575d618.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/243ddff62e10b575d618.webp\" width=\"516\" height=\"578\" style=\"--source-width:254px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>DLSS 2.0</strong></p>\n<p>Graphics always come at a cost—an increasingly expensive one. As games demand more pixels rendered at once, hardware struggles to keep up, resulting in lower and unstable framerate. To ease the stress on hardware, an AI-assisted rendering feature called DLSS was developed. Version 2.0 was just released in March.</p>\n<p>DLSS stands for Deep Learning Super Sampling. This feature uses AI to construct sharp, higher resolution images with fewer pixel. In short, it upscales a game’s resolution with an AI.</p>\n<p><strong>DLSS is currently only available to GeForce RTX GPUs, but the prospects it brings to game rendering foretell a rifer future.</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Motion matching</strong></p>\n<p>Oftentimes, our definition of <em>being realistic</em> is shackled by sheer graphical fidelity: we regard an asset as realistic when it resembles the world beyond our monitors. But realism is not simply found in 8K textures, gazillion polygons, and complex shaders. Realism can be found in a raise of an eyebrow or a change in posture. Realism relishes in animation.</p>\n<p>In the E3 2018 demo of <em>The Last of Us 2</em>, what impressed people wasn’t the detail in Ellie’s character model but the brief moment when she cocked her pistol before firing. Amongst the rising suspicion in the community—<em>It was staged</em>, they thought.</p>\n<p>It was not.</p>\n<p>Naughty Dog just simply have a new approach to animating human locomotion called “motion matching”.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/f03611233345d00496c6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/f03611233345d00496c6.webp\" width=\"843\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:313px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/74bb600cadaf78ecebd9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/74bb600cadaf78ecebd9.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"986\" style=\"--source-width:254px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Using mocap footage for reference is not an outlandish idea to videogame animators. However, for years, they’ve just been using it to compare and contrast with their work.</p>\n<p>Motion matching is about building a data library full of those references within a game. By implementing a smart raycasting system on each character, the game can transfer these mocap data directly to their rigs, allowing them to perform unique contextual animations—such as stumbling realistically against a wall. So far, there are 3 known games using this technique: <em>For Honor</em>, <em>UFC3</em>, and<em> The Last of Us 2</em>.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Ray tracing</strong></p>\n<p>Light is complex, and video games always struggle to replicate its complexity. If you are lighting artist, you probably know that better than anyone. To showcase how sophisticated (and difficult) light can be, we can look at mirrors.</p>\n<p>Mirrors are an opportune visual instrument. When used correctly, it can elevate a scene both aesthetically and emotionally. But as video games evolve graphically, we see less and less mirrors in them—particularly functional ones. Why?</p>\n<p>To put it simply, mirrors work in real life because we have real light. Games don’t have that luxury, so old games created a viewport on the other side of a level to trick us. There was actually no reflection at all. This is why ray tracing will be important feature for next-gen graphics.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/19a3b168c4426ca58cc1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/19a3b168c4426ca58cc1.webp\" width=\"873\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:313px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><em>Control</em> is one of the first titles to successfully implement ray tracing. The result is astounding. As you can see in the picture, the glass panels pick up every reflection in the scene. And if that is not enough to sell you on the technology, you can compare <em>Minecraft</em> before and after using ray tracing. You won’t be disappointed.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/b26f1d5a2194ec2dc442.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/b26f1d5a2194ec2dc442.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:254px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/2e7deb86f2c3121b3467.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/2e7deb86f2c3121b3467.webp\" width=\"940\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:254px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>SSD</strong></p>\n<p>The SSD is responsible for loading assets into the RAM, so the console can display them on screen. Next-gen SSDs from both the Xbox team and the PlayStation team are extremely fast, especially when compared to the current generation. That means game developers can reduce or cut load times entirely.</p>\n<p>Let’s take an example, many AAA games have walking segments—characters slowing down and having conversation while the player has to push <em>move forward</em>. It is like an interactive cutscene with very little interaction. These segments are tolerable on the first playthrough, but not on the subsequent ones. However, it’s no the developers’ fault. They just wanted to mask the loading of assets in a diegetic way. Now with the SSD’s super fast load speed, they can get rid of these segments.</p>\n<p><em>Star Wars Jedi: Fallen Order</em> has many “squeeze-through” sequences to accommodate asset-loading. However, it doesn’t always work out.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>In terms of game design, the SSD will allow developers to create more seamless, vast environments. For example, <em>Marvel’s Spider-Man PS4</em> had to put a limit on player’s speed, so the system can load assets into the environments sufficiently. In the PS5 demo, everything loads 10 times faster, which means Spidey can (technically) swing 10 times faster now.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/bf5e03f27783ab11e387.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/bf5e03f27783ab11e387.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"282\" style=\"--source-width:254px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>In terms of visuals, the SSD’s quickness can ensure that assets in the playable area will have the highest Level of Detail; as the player moves, it will load and unload the LOD accordingly to provide the best visual experience. Therefore, it will be easier to handle large crowds with next-gen systems.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/a0241e8477e6b346c9de.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/a0241e8477e6b346c9de.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:254px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>“All around you”</strong></p>\n<p>One of the highlights in the PlayStation team’s deep dive into the PS5 was their 3D audio implementation. This technique basically provides sounds all around the user, relative to their environment. While games are mostly about looks and feels, sounds can certainly enhance the immersiveness, especially in horror titles.</p>\n<p>The Xbox team hasn’t commented on their sound system, so this might be a unique selling point for the PS5.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/b86f280a7e19f2a28874.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/b86f280a7e19f2a28874.webp\" width=\"802\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:313px\" alt=\"Illustration 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/cookies-inline/ce9582287127eec718c6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/cookies-inline/ce9582287127eec718c6.webp\" width=\"923\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:254px\" alt=\"Illustration 13\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Unification</strong></p>\n<p>While next-gen games are always exciting, games from previous generations still have their charms. Backward compatibility is one of the most highly requested features for any upcoming console—for good reason. With each generation lasts from 6 – 8 years, gamers will have had dozens of titles in their library, games that they want to revisit—or even play for the first time.</p>\n<p>Xbox’s Smart Delivery system is a very customer-friendly feature. It allows people to upgrade their Xbox One games to Xbox Series X versions for free. This means people don’t have to worry about repurchasing games to experience next-gen enhancements.</p>\n<p>PS5 has a less impressive approach as the PlayStation team only hand select top games based on their database to enable next-gen compatability. So, not every PS4 game is playable on PS5. However, they promised that there will hundreds of playable titles at launch—with next-gen enhancements.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>See you next-gen</strong></p>\n<p>The coming of new generation of consoles is always exciting. It seems like the 9<sup>th</sup> generation will have a bigger jump than those before it: games won’t just simply improve in terms of how they look, but also how they play and sound. Even though some titles will remain exclusive to certain consoles, it is safe to say that we are going to witness some truly magnificent evolution in the next few years.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>";
export const images = ["assets/cookies-inline/19a3b168c4426ca58cc1.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/243ddff62e10b575d618.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/258703a51f66f1db1c9b.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/2e7deb86f2c3121b3467.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/37209ba42ddb64f90270.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/74bb600cadaf78ecebd9.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/a0241e8477e6b346c9de.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/a216b4690aae539dfd77.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/b26f1d5a2194ec2dc442.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/b86f280a7e19f2a28874.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/bf5e03f27783ab11e387.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/ce9582287127eec718c6.webp", "assets/cookies-inline/f03611233345d00496c6.webp"];
